---
import Article from "../../../layouts/Article.astro";
import KeyTakeaway from "../../../components/KeyTakeaway.astro";
import InfoBox from "../../../components/InfoBox.astro";
import StepList from "../../../components/StepList.astro";
import Step from "../../../components/Step.astro";
import QuickFact from "../../../components/QuickFact.astro";
import ComparisonTable from "../../../components/ComparisonTable.astro";
---

<Article
  title="How to Play Flip 7: Complete Rules in 5 Minutes"
  description="Every rule in the box, explained in the order you actually need them at the table."
  game="flip-7"
  gameTitle="Flip 7"
  category="Rules"
  product="flip-7-organizer"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    Flip 7 is a push-your-luck card game where you keep asking for cards until
    you either bank your points or lose them all. It teaches in about two
    minutes, which is exactly why it exploded — but the action cards trip up
    almost every new group. Here's the whole game, in order.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="Collect number cards without ever flipping a duplicate. Bank your points by staying, or bust and score zero. Seven different numbers earns a 15-point bonus. First to 200 wins." />

  <div class="my-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <QuickFact label="Players" value="3–18" icon="👥" />
    <QuickFact label="Time" value="~20 min" icon="⏱️" />
    <QuickFact label="Ages" value="8+" icon="🎯" />
    <QuickFact label="Target score" value="200" icon="🏆" />
  </div>

  <h2>What's in the Box</h2>

  <p>
    Ninety-four cards, and that's it. Knowing the breakdown matters more than
    you'd think, because it drives every decision you make later.
  </p>

  <ComparisonTable title="Flip 7 card breakdown">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Card type</th>
          <th>Count</th>
          <th>What it is</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Number cards</strong></td>
          <td>79</td>
          <td>0 through 12 — each number appears as many times as its value</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Modifier cards</strong></td>
          <td>6</td>
          <td>+2, +4, +6, +8, +10 and ×2</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Action cards</strong></td>
          <td>9</td>
          <td>Three each of Freeze, Flip Three and Second Chance</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </ComparisonTable>

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="The number distribution is the whole game">
    There are twelve 12s, eleven 11s, ten 10s… down to a single 1 and a single 0.
    High cards score more, but they're also the ones most likely to show up again
    and bust you. Every strategic decision in Flip 7 comes back to this.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Setting Up</h2>

  <p>
    Shuffle all 94 cards into one deck. Pick a dealer. Grab paper for scores —
    you'll be tracking cumulative totals across several rounds. That's the entire
    setup, which is a large part of the appeal.
  </p>

  <h2>Playing a Round</h2>

  <StepList title="The turn sequence">
    <Step number={1} title="The dealer offers a card">
      Starting to the dealer's left, the dealer goes around the table dealing one
      card at a time to each player who is still in the round.
    </Step>
    <Step number={2} title="You choose: Hit or Stay">
      Say <strong>Hit</strong> and take the card. Say <strong>Stay</strong> and
      you're out of the round — but you keep every point in front of you.
    </Step>
    <Step number={3} title="Duplicate numbers bust you">
      If a card you flip matches a number card you already have, you bust. All
      your cards for the round are discarded and you score <strong>zero</strong>.
      Modifier and action cards can never bust you.
    </Step>
    <Step number={4} title="Seven unique numbers ends the round">
      Get seven different number cards in front of you and you've hit
      <strong>Flip 7</strong>. You score a 15-point bonus and the round ends
      immediately for everyone.
    </Step>
    <Step number={5} title="Score and reshuffle">
      Everyone still in adds up their round score. Discard all cards, the deal
      passes left, and a new round begins.
    </Step>
  </StepList>

  <p>
    A round ends when every player has either stayed, busted, or someone hits
    Flip 7. Players who busted score nothing; players who stayed bank what they
    had.
  </p>

  <h2>Scoring a Round</h2>

  <p>
    Add the face values of your number cards, apply any modifiers you collected,
    and add 15 if you hit Flip 7. So a player holding a 12, a 9, a 4 and a +8 scores
    12 + 9 + 4 + 8 = <strong>33</strong>.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="warning" title="The ×2 trap">
    The ×2 card doubles the total of your <em>number cards only</em> — it does
    not double your + modifiers, and it does not double the 15-point Flip 7
    bonus. Almost every new group gets this wrong on the first play.
    We break the whole thing down in
    <a href="/games/flip-7/flip-7-scoring-explained/">Flip 7 scoring explained</a>.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>The Three Action Cards</h2>

  <p>
    These are the only genuinely fiddly part of the game, and they're what makes
    it more than a dice roll.
  </p>

  <div class="my-6 space-y-3">
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-sky-100 text-sky-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">FREEZE</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Force someone to stop</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Give it to any player still in the round — including yourself. That player immediately stays and banks whatever they have. Brutal when someone is sitting on six unique numbers.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-rose-100 text-rose-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">FLIP THREE</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Force three more cards</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">The target immediately flips three cards, one at a time. If they bust partway through, it stops there. If they hit Flip 7, the round ends.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">SECOND CHANCE</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Survive one bust</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Keep it in front of you. The first time you'd bust, discard the duplicate and the Second Chance instead, and carry on. You can only ever hold one.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="Action cards don't count toward Flip 7">
    Only <strong>number</strong> cards count toward the seven you need. Modifiers
    and action cards sit in your row but never get you closer to the bonus.
  </InfoBox>

  <p>
    The targeting rules and the awkward cases — what happens if you're dealt a
    second Second Chance, or an action card when nobody else is left — are
    covered in
    <a href="/games/flip-7/flip-7-action-cards-explained/">the action card guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Winning</h2>

  <p>
    Keep playing rounds until at least one player reaches <strong>200 points</strong>.
    Finish the round you're in — you don't stop mid-round — and the highest total
    at that point wins.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="Teaching it to a new table">
    Don't explain the action cards up front. Deal the first round and explain
    each one the moment it appears. People understand Freeze instantly when it's
    being used on them, and you'll have the whole table playing inside three
    minutes.
  </InfoBox>
</Article>
